04 = 0.0064 ≈ 0.006 ✓\n\nQ4 = 1.25 × Q3:\n- Variant 1: 1.25 × 2.5 = 3.125 ✓\n- Variants 2-4: 1.25 × 4 = 5.00 ✓\n\nSo the models are four variants. The nominal diameter is 20 (DN20). Interesting that the photo shows DN25 but the table says nominal diameter 20 — that's the photograph example, which may differ. I'll extract what's in the table.\n\nActually wait — also from the photo: \"DN25 Q3=2.5m3/h, R=1600\" — that's the example photograph text, showing another variant possibly (DN25, Q3=2.5, R=1600). But this is a photograph caption, likely illustrating the display, not a certified model spec necessarily. I should be careful. The photo also shows H.V. 196 U=3,6V (battery voltage 3.6V?), MAP 16 (max pressure 16 bar?), T50 (temperature class 50°C).\n\nSince this is a fragment (part 3 of 3), I should only extract content present in this fragment. The instructions say: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nSo I should extract:\n\n- certificate: number R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06 (visible in the fragment). Scheme A (from \"A\" in the number). Page total: 7 pages (\"Page 6 of 7 pages\"). \n\n- issuing_authority: Český metrologický institut (from the stamp). The stamp is described: \"circular blue stamp — 'Český metrologický institut' with Czech lion emblem\". Hmm, should I include this? It's from a stamp description. I could include name \"Český metrologický institut\" but no address etc. Probably reasonable to include minimal info. Actually, the oiml_issuer_id for CMI is... I shouldn't fabricate. I'll just include the name.\n\n- manufacturers[]: JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A. (from the table). No address in this fragment.\n\n- certified_type: category \"Water meter\" (it's a water meter — R49 covers water meters; text says \"water meter types Smartio\"). type_designations: [\"Smartio\"]. Description could mention securing/sealing.\n\n- model_f