   {\n      \"name\": \"JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Smartio\"],\n    \"description\": \"Smartio water meters, nominal diameter DN20. Securing components: the meters are sealed by connecting the plastic seal on the plastic meter cover; the plastic seal is part of the meter body in the form of a plastic frame that holds the display glass on the meter body, so the meter cannot be accessed without damaging the seal. LCD display shows volume (m³ or GAL) and flow rate (m³/h or GPM), with indicators for tariff number, flow direction, radio connection status, leakage, test mode, air in pipe, system alarm and battery level. Figure 2 shows an example meter label: DN25, Q3=2.5 m³/h, R=1600, U=3.6 V, MAP 16, T50.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Smartio\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN20_Q3=2.5_R630\", \"label\": \"Smartio DN20, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h, R 630\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter_mm\": 20, \"q3_m3h\": 2.5, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 630}},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, attributes keys — keep simple: {\"nominal_diameter\": 20, \"q3\": 2.5, \"ratio\": 630}. Units implied. Maybe skip attributes to avoid mess, just model_id and label. The schema says attributes{} optional. I'll include minimal attributes with plain values (they're duplicated in model_level anyway). Actually to reduce redundancy risk, I'll include attributes since schema shows it.\n\nLet me reconsider member_state: The certificate field member_state — the issuing authority CMI is Czech. Including \"Czech Republic\" — I'm fairly confident. But is it in the fragment? The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut\". Inferring member_state from that is reasonable. I'll include it.\n\nShould I include issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id? Not present. Omit.\n\nLet me double-check the certificate number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06\". Yes.\n\nPage total: \"Page